# Without this file, `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile copies the ENTIRE working
# directory into the image — including .env with real secrets, and .venv.
#
# Two consequences, both real:
#   * the built image carried /app/.env, so every secret in it shipped to ECR
#     and into production, readable by anyone who can pull the image;
#   * the image was 1.49GB, almost all of it a Linux-incompatible .venv that
#     is never used because dependencies are installed from requirements.txt.
#
# It also made the image a dishonest test environment: settings silently read
# /app/.env, so a container run looked correctly configured even when nothing
# had been passed in — the opposite of CI, which has no .env at all.

# ==================== Secrets ====================
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
token.json

# ==================== Virtualenv ====================
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ==================== Python ====================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# ==================== Tooling / caches ====================
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ==================== VCS / CI / IDE ====================
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# ==================== Local data ====================
*.sqlite3
*.db
dev.db
logs/
*.log
celerybeat-schedule*
